{"pagination":{"maxPageableSet":"10000","numFound":1,"query":"name=Mez&limit=10&title=Jesus and John Wayne","limit":10,"start":0},"items":{"mods":{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"title":"Jesus and John Wayne ","subTitle":"how white evangelicals corrupted a faith and fractured a nation "},"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":"Du Mez, Kristin Kobes","role":[{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}},{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"author"}}]},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"marcgt","#text":"bibliography"},{"@authority":"rdacontent","#text":"text"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Informational works"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Church history"},{"@authority":"lcgft","#text":"Informational works"},{"@authority":"rvmgf","#text":"Documents d'information."}],"originInfo":[{"place":{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"nyu"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"New York (State)"}]},"dateIssued":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2020"},"copyrightDate":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2020"},"issuance":"monographic","edition":"First edition."},{"@eventType":"publication","place":{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"New York, NY :"}},"agent":{"namePart":"Liveright Publishing Corporation, a division of W.W. Norton & Company,","role":{"roleTerm":"publisher"}},"dateIssued":[2020]}],"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":[{"form":[{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},{"@type":"media","@authority":"rdamedia","#text":"unmediated"},{"@type":"carrier","@authority":"rdacarrier","#text":"volume"}],"extent":"x, 356 pages : illustrations, portraits ; 25 cm"},{"form":{"@type":"Illustrative content","#text":"illustration"}},{"form":{"@type":"Illustrative content","#text":"portrait"}}],"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"\"A scholar of American Christianity answers perhaps the most bewildering question of our time: Why are evangelicals \"the Donald's\" most fervent supporters? Donald Trump is a libertine who lacks even basic knowledge of the Christian faith. Yet in 2016 he won 81 percent of the white evangelical vote, and continues to rely on white evangelicals as his base of support. While we assume the religious right has pragmatic reasons for backing Trump, in truth he represents the fulfillment of evangelicals' most deeply held values. As historian Kristin Kobes Du Mez demonstrates, American evangelicals have worked for decades to replace the Jesus of the Gospels with an idol of rugged masculinity and Christian nationalism-or, in the words of one modern chaplain, with \"a spiritual badass.\" Trump is hardly the first flashy celebrity to capture evangelicals' hearts and minds, having followed the path blazed by, among others, John Wayne, Oliver North, and Mel Gibson. A revelatory account of a uniquely influential subculture, Jesus and John Wayne incisively reveals why evangelicals have rallied behind patriarchal power and the least- Christian president in American history\"-- Provided by publisher"},"tableOfContents":{"@type":"Contents","#text":"Introduction -- Saddling up -- John Wayne will save your ass -- God's gift to man -- Discipline and command -- Slaves and soldiers -- Going for the jugular -- The greatest American hero -- War for the soul -- Tender warriors -- No more Christian nice guy -- Holy balls -- Pilgrim's progress in camo -- Why we want to kill you -- Spiritual badasses -- A new high priest -- Evangelical Mulligans: a history -- Conclusion."},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Kristin Kobes Du Mez."},{"@type":"bibliography","#text":"Includes bibliographical references (pages 311-342) and index."}],"subject":[{"geographicCode":{"@authority":"marcgac","#text":"n-us---"}},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Evangelicalism","geographic":"United States"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Christianity and culture"